{"records":[{"id":"s83f5y9ntt","title":"DUB3 promotes BET inhibitor resistance and cancer progression through deubiquitinating BRD4. Jin et al","description":"The bromodomain and extra-terminal domain (BET) protein BRD4 is emerging as a promising anticancer therapeutic target. However, resistance to BET inhibitors often occurs, and it has been linked to aberrant degradation of BRD4 protein in cancer. Here, we demonstrate that the deubiquitinase DUB3 binds to BRD4 and promotes its deubiquitination and stabilization. Expression of DUB3 is transcriptionally repressed by the NCOR2-HDAC10 complex. The NCOR2 gene is frequently deleted in castration-resistant prostate cancer patient specimens, and loss of NCOR2 induces elevation of DUB3 and BRD4 proteins in cancer cells. DUB3-proficient prostate cancer cells are resistant to the BET inhibitor JQ1 in vitro and in mice, but this effect is diminished by DUB3 inhibitory agents such as CDK4/6 inhibitor in a RB-independent manner. Our findings identify a previously unrecognized mechanism causing BRD4 upregulation and drug resistance, suggesting that DUB3 is a viable therapeutic target to overcome BET inhibitor resistance in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-13T19:55:44.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s83f5y9ntt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s83f5y9ntt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haojie","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunoprecipitation","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xbx248k5ft","title":"DYRK1A-TGFβ Signaling Axis Determines Sensitivity to OXPHOS Inhibition in Hepatocellular Carcinoma","description":"Intervening in mitochondrial OXPHOS has emerged as a potential therapeutic strategy for certain types of cancers. Employing kinome-based CRISPR screen, we find that knockout of DYRK1A synergizes with OXPHOS inhibitor IACS-010759 in liver cancer cells. Targeting DYRK1A combined with OXPHOS inhibitors activates TGFβ signaling, which is crucial for OXPHOS inhibition-triggered cell death. Mechanistically, upregulation of glutamine transporter SLC1A5 transcription compensates for the increased glutamine requirement upon OXPHOS inhibition. DYRK1A directly phosphorylates SMAD3 Thr132, thereby suppressing the negative impact of TGFβ signaling on transcription of SLC1A5, leading to intrinsic resistance of liver cancer cells to OXPHOS inhibition. Moreover, we demonstrate the therapeutic efficacy of IACS-010759 in combination with DYRK1A inhibition in multiple liver cancer models, including xenograftes, patient-derived xenograftes and spontaneous tumor model. Our study elucidates how the DYRK1A-TGFβ signaling axis controls the response of tumor cells to OXPHOS inhibition and provides valuable insights into targeting OXPHOS for liver cancer therapy. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-13T15:22:10.239Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xbx248k5ft","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xbx248k5ft"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ying","last_name":"cao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vt434wb4b4","title":"Anti-CRISPR phages cooperate to overcome CRISPR-Cas immunity","description":"Some phages encode anti-CRISPR (acr) genes, which antagonize bacterial CRISPR-Cas immune systems by binding components of its machinery, but it is less clear how deployment of these acr genes impacts phage replication and epidemiology. Here we demonstrate that bacteria with CRISPR-Cas resistance are still partially immune to Acr-encoding phage. As a consequence, Acr-phages often need to cooperate in order to overcome CRISPR resistance, with a first phage taking down the host CRISPR-Cas immune system to allow a second Acr-phage to successfully replicate. This cooperation leads to epidemiological tipping points in which the initial density of Acr-phage tips the balance from phage extinction to a phage epidemic. Furthermore, both higher levels of CRISPR-Cas immunity and weaker Acr activities shift the tipping points towards higher phage densities. Collectively these data help to understand how interactions between phage-encoded immune suppressors and the CRISPR systems they target shape bacteria-phage population dynamics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-05-22T22:30:09.605Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vt434wb4b4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vt434wb4b4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mariann","last_name":"Landsberger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sylvain","last_name":"Gandon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Meaden","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Clare","last_name":"Rollie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hélène","last_name":"Chabas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anne","last_name":"Chevallereau","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Angus","last_name":"Buckling","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Edze R.","last_name":"Westra","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stineke","last_name":"van Houte","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Epidemiology","Biological Sciences","Bacteria","Bacteriophage","Immunosuppression","Resistance (Fungal Pathogenesis)","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nnxh5d35nd","title":"Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen identified KLF11 as a druggable suppressor for sarcoma cancer stem cells","description":"The original, unprocessed data alongside the paper","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-31T01:39:45.722Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nnxh5d35nd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nnxh5d35nd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Le","last_name":"Qu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"257psct2ds","title":"Low dose enzalutamide in the Martinique","description":"Data and script supporting the analyses of the manuscript: Non-inferior survival and enhanced longevity with low-dose enzalutamide in prostate cancer.\nThe study has no funding. Authors will respond to queries to the best of their availability.\n\nData freeze as of May 2, 2025.\n\nMajor update August 9, 2026.\nPrevious uploads contained contact dates that could be used for identification.\nThe new files replace all dates with the time relative to the date enzalutamide was first prescribed, set as the origin 0. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-10T15:29:15.449Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/257psct2ds","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/257psct2ds"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Vincent","last_name":"Vinh-Hung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Prostate Cancer","Toxicity","Androgen Inhibitor","Dose Response","Drug Dose"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d5vw9sjcrv","title":"A target expression threshold dictates invader defense  and autoimmunity by CRISPR-Cas13","description":"Immune systems must recognize and clear foreign invaders without eliciting autoimmunity. CRISPR-Cas immune systems in prokaryotes manage this task by following two criteria: extensive guide:target complementarity and a defined target-flanking motif. Here we report an additional requirement for RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 systems: expression of the target transcript exceeding a threshold. This finding is based on targeting endogenous non-essential transcripts, which rarely elicited dormancy through collateral RNA degradation. Instead, eliciting dormancy required over-expressing targeted transcripts above a threshold. A genome-wide screen confirmed target expression levels as the principal determinant of cytotoxic autoimmunity and revealed that the threshold shifts with the guide:target pair. This expression threshold ensured defense against a lytic bacteriophage yet allowed tolerance of a targeted beneficial gene expressed from an invading plasmid. These findings establish target expression levels as a third criterion for immune defense by RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas systems, buffering against autoimmunity and distinguishing pathogenic and benign invaders.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-08T11:55:48.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d5vw9sjcrv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d5vw9sjcrv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elena","last_name":"Vialetto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanying","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Collins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katharina","last_name":"Wandera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lars","last_name":"Barquist","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chase","last_name":"Beisel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis","Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x29rjbpk8r","title":"Raw Data for \"A chemical screen identifies PRMT5 as a therapeutic vulnerability for paclitaxel-resistant triple-negative breast cancer\"","description":"This file contains the following raw data for \"A chemical screen identifies PRMT5 as a therapeutic vulnerability for paclitaxel-resistant triple-negative breast cancer\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-24T09:06:29.878Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x29rjbpk8r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x29rjbpk8r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sheyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biologicals"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135141,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":174,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135141,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":134552,"restricted":66,"embargoed":523},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2238},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1388},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":620},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":465},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":370},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":305},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":263}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":132509},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4